    "id": "train-3538",
    "question": "On a four-day trip, Carrie drove 135 miles the first day, 124 miles more the second day, 159 miles the third day, and 189 miles the fourth day. If she had to charge her phone every 106 miles, how many times did she charge her phone for the whole trip?",
    "gold": "7",
    "answer": "Carrie drove 135 + 124 = <<135+124=259>>259 miles on the second day\nCarrie drove 135 + 259 + 159 + 189 = <<135+259+159+189=742>>742 miles for the whole trip.\nCarries charged her phone 742 / 106 = <<742/106=7>>7 times.\n#### 7"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many times Carrie charged her phone for the whole trip, we need to calculate the total distance she drove over the four days and then determine how many times she charged her phone based on the distance between charges.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the distance Carrie drove each day:\n- The first day, she drove 135 miles.\n- The second day, she drove 124 miles more than the first day, which is \\(135 + 124 = 259\\) miles.\n- The third day, she drove 159 miles.\n- The fourth day, she drove 189 miles.\n\nNext, we sum these distances to find the total distance driven over the four days:\n\\[\n135 + 259 + 159 + 189 = 742 \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nCarrie charges her phone every 106 miles. To find out how many times she charged her phone, we divide the total distance by 106 and round up to the nearest whole number (since she will have to charge her phone one more time if the total distance is not a multiple of 106):\n\\[\n\\frac{742}{106} = 7\n\\]\nSince 742 is exactly divisible by 106, Carrie did not need to charge her phone any additional time after the 7th charge.\n\nTherefore, the total number of times Carrie charged her phone is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
